%I A398785 #10 Aug 15 2026 17:01:11
%S A398785 1,2,3,4,5,8,14,17,20,22,32,37,55,64,73
%N A398785 a(n) is the least N such that some n-element subset of {1,...,N} contains no pairwise distinct x, y, z, w with x + 3*y = 2*z + 2*w.
%C A398785 Writing f(N) for the maximum size of such a subset of {1,...,N} (Problem 16 in Ben Green's list of 100 open problems; the question was asked by Ruzsa), a(n) is the least N with f(N) = n, and conversely f(N) is the largest n with a(n) <= N.
%C A398785 It is known that sqrt(N) << f(N) << N*exp(-c*(log N)^(1/7)) for some c > 0; the lower bound is due to Ruzsa and the upper bound to Schoen and Sisask.
%C A398785 By minimality, every witness set for a(n) contains N itself.
%C A398785 Note that z and w are interchangeable in the equation but x and y are not.
%D A398785 I. Z. Ruzsa, Solving a linear equation in a set of integers. I. Acta Arith. 65 (1993), no. 3, 259-282.
%D A398785 T. Schoen and O. Sisask, Roth's theorem for four variables and additive structures in sums of sparse sets, Forum of Mathematics, Sigma 4 (2016), e5, 28 pages.
%H A398785 Ben Green, <a href="https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/greenbj/papers/open-problems.pdf">100 open problems</a>, Problem 16.
%e A398785 a(6) = 8: the 6-element set {1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 8} contains no solution to x + 3y = 2z + 2w in pairwise distinct elements, and no 6-element subset of {1,...,7} has this property.
%K A398785 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A398785 1,2
%A A398785 _Paweł Kwaczyński_, Aug 09 2026
